- Existing N1–N5 records remain unchanged.
- The updated CSV can be read by the current E6 analysis scripts.

Do not fabricate packet counts to make packet-loss percentages appear internally consistent. If packet counts are required to validate a percentage, report that limitation.

## 5. E6 Analysis for Research Publication

Using the available data, produce a reproducible analysis of:

- Distance versus average RTT
- Distance versus maximum RTT
- Distance versus RSSI
- Packet loss versus distance
- TX bitrate or throughput only where actual measurements exist

Use clear axis labels, units, legends, and publication-quality formatting. Report the number of available observations and explicitly state which measurements are missing.

Discuss the observed trends without claiming causation that the data cannot establish. Do not claim that 50 m is the maximum communication range: the supplied results alone do not establish the distance at which the link fails. Do not invent confidence intervals, statistical significance, repeated trials, or reliability guarantees.

Keep measured data separate from any simulated or illustrative data. Any simulation must be stored separately and clearly labeled.

